            <div xml:id="d350">

                <pb facs="0453" n="373" sameAs="frus:frus1930v01/pg373"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">715.1715/390: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lay</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-09-18T20:00:00-06:00">September 18, 1930—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 19—12:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>87. The following telegram was sent to the Legation at Managua: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“September 18, 8 p.m. Referring to your telegram of September 16, 2
                                p.m.,<anchor n="97" xml:id="d350-f1"/> stating that full text of the
                            boundary protocol, proposed by the Government of Honduras and
                            transmitted with my air mail letter of August 17, is accepted by the
                            Government of Nicaragua with the omission of article 5. I take great
                            pleasure in informing you that after explaining to the President that
                            the omission of article 5 would improve chances of ratification in the
                            Nicaraguan Congress he agreed to this text of the protocol in full
                            without article 5.</p>
                        <p>Believing the moment opportune and for reasons you mention, I urged him
                            to reconsider his previous objection to signing in Managua. A copy of a
                            note from the Honduranean Foreign Office to this Legation agreeing to
                            text of protocol in full with omission of article 5 and stating that
                            Honduranean Government would have no objection to signing in Managua
                            will be sent you by marine air mail tomorrow”.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lay</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
